\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Cross-country cumulative monetary policy shocks, standardised, January 2005 to December 2022. Each series is divided by its full-sample standard deviation of nonzero shocks before cumulating, so the y-axis is in units of standard deviations; scales are comparable across countries despite different native units in the source data (percentage-point surprises for US and UK, unit-variance PC score for AU). The three traces are \texttt  {bauer\_swanson} (United States, MPS\_ORTH), \texttt  {ukmpd} Target factor (United Kingdom), and \texttt  {hambur\_haque\_au} action factor (Australia). Shaded bands mark the global financial crisis (September 2008 to June 2009) and the COVID-19 monetary response (March to June 2020). The three central banks share the direction of policy around major global events but differ sharply in timing and magnitude.}}{6}{figure.caption.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:cross-country}{{3}{6}{Cross-country cumulative monetary policy shocks, standardised, January 2005 to December 2022. Each series is divided by its full-sample standard deviation of nonzero shocks before cumulating, so the y-axis is in units of standard deviations; scales are comparable across countries despite different native units in the source data (percentage-point surprises for US and UK, unit-variance PC score for AU). The three traces are \texttt {bauer\_swanson} (United States, MPS\_ORTH), \texttt {ukmpd} Target factor (United Kingdom), and \texttt {hambur\_haque\_au} action factor (Australia). Shaded bands mark the global financial crisis (September 2008 to June 2009) and the COVID-19 monetary response (March to June 2020). The three central banks share the direction of policy around major global events but differ sharply in timing and magnitude}{figure.caption.4}{}}
